    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Choosing between a metal roof and asphalt shingles in Utah depends on your budget, how long you plan to stay in your home, and how your roof handles local weather like heavy mountain snow and intense summer UV rays.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "depends on your budget, how long you plan to stay in your home, and how your roof handles local weather like heavy mountain snow and intense summer UV rays"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          3,
          0,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost and Lifespan"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: Costs $8,000 to $18,000 installed and lasts 15 to 25 years\nin Utah's high-UV, freeze-thaw climate. Upfront costs are lower, making it ideal if you plan to sell soon.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing: Costs $16,000 to $40,000+ upfront (roughly 2 to 3 times the price of asphalt), but lasts 40 to 70 years. Over a long time, it can cost less per year."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Weather Performance in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Snow and Ice: Metal takes the lead by naturally shedding heavy winter snow and preventing ice dams, though snow retention brackets are needed over doorways. Asphalt can trap moisture and ice during repeated freeze-thaw cycles, shortening its lifespan.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Wind and Sun: Properly installed metal handles high canyon gusts (70\u201380 mph) and intense UV rays without cracking. Asphalt can experience granule loss, curling, and cracking faster under extreme temperature swings and high altitude sun.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hail and Maintenance: High-grade architectural asphalt shingles absorb hail impact well and are easy to patch. Metal resists cracking from hail but can show cosmetic dents from large stones. Forum users on\nshare a consensus that upgrading to a thicker metal gauge (like 24-gauge over 26-gauge) significantly helps prevent visible waviness (oil canning) and improves hail resistance.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.reddit.com/r/homeowners/comments/1l994vi/the_great_debate_shingles_or_metal_roof/"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          7,
          8
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To see a side-by-side quality and durability breakdown of these two materials, watch this video:",
